The values here are invented — no real issue, user, organisation or token appears
in these files. Only the structure is borrowed.

## Which of these have been confirmed against a real Tracker

`every_fixture_still_has_the_shape_tracker_answers_with`, in `tests/live.rs`,
asks the real API once per endpoint and fails on a key a fixture claims and
Tracker no longer returns. Everything it can reach has been confirmed that way.

Seven of them used to be out of its reach, because no organisation had any of the
thing they describe and an empty array agrees with every fixture ever written
(#78). They are reachable now:
`one_of_everything_the_fixture_audit_cannot_otherwise_reach` makes one of each —
a queue version, a component with a lead, a local field with an options provider,
a board with a sprint, an issue template, a link out of Tracker, and a required
field on the queue — and then checks the fixture against what comes back.

All seven have been confirmed that way against a real organisation. Run it before
trusting any of them again after an edit:

    YTCLI_PROFILE=… YTCLI_TEST_QUEUE=… just test-live

What that run also established, none of which is in the API reference:

  * `POST /v3/issueTemplates` exists. `fieldTemplates` is an object of field to
    value; the array form the reference uses elsewhere is refused, and so is an
    empty one.
  * A template made through the API carries no `createdBy`. The first element of
    `issue_templates.json` is the shape Tracker answered with; the second keeps
    the `createdBy` variant, which is still only borrowed.
  * `GET /v3/queues/{key}/fields` is the queue's *required* fields, which is why
    it answers `[]` in an organisation that has plenty of fields. `PATCH` on the
    same path sets them, and takes a bare array of field ids.
  * Anything versioned — a component, a local field — takes its version in the
    query (`?version=N`), not in the body: sent as a field it answers
    "version: Incorrect data format", which reads like the value is wrong rather
    than the place.
  * A remote link needs an application registered in the organisation, and not
    every one of them accepts a link: Wiki answers 403 and Messenger 500, while
    GitLab works. `GET /v3/applications` lists the candidates.
  * Listing endpoints lag a second or two behind the write that fed them, so a
    fixture checked immediately after a write can look like it has drifted.

`comments_html.json` is the exception in the other direction: it was recorded
from a real organisation, sanitised of names and ids, and is the evidence for
what `markupType` does (#77).
